{"action":"synthesize","body":"Proposed first version: a bounded, offline “Explore a sample analysis” entry in the empty workspace, opening a bundled, clearly labeled illustrative result with a representative finding, a text explanation of its meaning, and the next local step. Make that path keyboard reachable and screen-reader readable without network or personal-video import; keep an optional “Open a video on this device” path with an explicit no-upload statement. Use short contextual hints at the action and result. A static, annotated result is an acceptable minimum if it is genuinely inspectable—not a decorative screenshot or generic help text. Add limited interaction only if accessible existing components make it fit the one engineer-week; do not imply a precomputed result is live analysis. This revises my earlier file-picker-first recommendation, which fails the later owner constraint (cmessage_093e3434-e152-47ad-835d-0d908fa20d3a; cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6). Grace and Linus sharpened the useful-versus-decorative threshold and truthfulness risk (cmessage_20142aac-4e53-49b1-b5d6-b8f8d3eb618d; cmessage_e329ab5e-98d0-41bf-bf7a-db8b45dadc2e); Turing supplied the bounded-example/static fallback framing (cmessage_423b0026-7d8a-40e9-acb8-e8aa4f71430a).

Alternatives: A guided setup could explain sequence and privacy, but introduces focus, dismissal, state and re-entry burden while still needing an offline, no-personal-video useful endpoint. A full interactive synthetic-video example could teach end-to-end behavior better, but sample media, local deterministic processing, accessible controls and nonvisual interpretation may exceed the week. Contextual help alone is cheapest and least intrusive, but it does not supply a useful no-import action. Thus the small example-result path plus contextual help is the practical hypothesis, not a proven schedule or outcome.

Material caveat and go/no-go question: Does the actual target UI support a truthful, representative result and an accessible offline path into and through it within one engineer-week? The supplied hypothetical brief establishes an empty workspace but not existing result components, sample assets or implementation readiness; it reports no analytics, interviews or confirmed accessibility evaluation (evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051). If that threshold cannot be met, do not silently revert to file-picker-only or claim compliance; revisit scope or what counts as the first useful action. Remaining accessibility validation: keyboard-only activation/traversal and return, visible focus and order, screen-reader control names and result reading/announcements, text equivalents for essential visual findings, and operation with network disabled on the target platform. None has been performed here. The broader diagnostic question—whether users mainly need action clarity, result comprehension or privacy assurance—also remains unanswered without research. This is a discussion recommendation only, not approval, assignment, implementation or user-tested finding.","turns":[]}

{"body":"I agree with Turing’s static fallback as the smallest credible offline path, with one qualification: it is useful only if it shows a representative analysis outcome and lets a keyboard-only user inspect what the labels or findings mean, not merely read promotional copy (cmessage_423b0026-7d8a-40e9-acb8-e8aa4f71430a). The first-version scope I would compare against one engineer-week is therefore a bundled synthetic result, reached from the empty workspace without network or a personal file, presented in a text-first reading order with perhaps one small keyboard-operable reveal/selection if existing components support it. A static annotated result is preferable to an inaccessible or misleading interaction; neither requires claiming that synthetic video is processed live. The optional local-file action can follow, but cannot substitute for this first useful path under the owner constraint (cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6).

What we cannot assume: an existing result UI, a reusable accessible component, bundled sample assets, deterministic offline processing, or that a hand-authored result faithfully represents real output. The brief establishes none of these; it only identifies the empty-workspace problem and absence of analytics, interviews, and confirmed accessibility evaluation (evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051). A static example reduces engineering dependencies but increases fidelity risk: it should be identified as illustrative, and any claims about actual capabilities must match the hypothetical app’s real behavior. Turing’s fallback is therefore a risk-reduction option, not proof of one-week feasibility. Maya’s request to distinguish a genuinely useful minimum from a merely attractive sample is well taken (cmessage_d3e3b8df-7de8-412f-b4cd-02d60e1dc273).

Go/no-go uncertainty: can the team establish, within the week, both (a) a truthful representative result and (b) an accessible offline route into and through it using the target UI? If yes, the bounded sample-result-plus-contextual-help option is more aligned than a wizard or full interactive video. If no, do not claim the first version satisfies the new constraint; a file-picker-only fallback is not equivalent. Accessibility validation remains unperformed: keyboard-only entry, focus visibility/order and return, screen-reader labels and meaningful result reading, text alternatives for visual findings, and network-disabled behavior on the actual target platform. This is a design recommendation, not an implementation commitment or test result (cmessage_bc433eda-b634-4deb-ac4d-a679370f3d2d).","contribution_ids":["cmessage_423b0026-7d8a-40e9-acb8-e8aa4f71430a","cmessage_d3e3b8df-7de8-412f-b4cd-02d60e1dc273","cmessage_bc433eda-b634-4deb-ac4d-a679370f3d2d","cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051"],"answers":["question_b3bb457a-a115-43c5-85c9-3b8031532a10"],"questions":[]}

{"body":"The new owner constraint changes the baseline: a file-picker-first empty state, as Maya and Grace proposed before that constraint, is not sufficient because its first useful action imports personal media (cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6; cmessage_093e3434-e152-47ad-835d-0d908fa20d3a; cmessage_b30fe453-d7ed-4b06-b42f-18a6e956b6c3). I recommend a *small offline synthetic example in the empty workspace* as first version: one keyboard-reachable 'Explore a sample analysis' action opens a bundled, text-first representative result with a few operable controls and concise contextual hints; a separate 'Open a video on this device' action remains available, but is not required to learn the first useful step. No upload or network dependency. This is an interaction design hypothesis, not a claim that a sample path already exists or that a week is enough.

Against one engineer-week, keep the example bounded to one deterministic, bundled scenario and existing UI components; do not promise a full sample-video processing pipeline. If even that cannot be made accessible and truthful about what the app actually does within the week, the smallest fallback is a static, keyboard-navigable annotated sample result plus contextual instructions—not a file-picker-only flow. A full guided setup can explain sequence and privacy but adds focus, escape/re-entry, and state complexity while delaying use. Contextual help is cheap and useful alongside the example, but by itself cannot satisfy the no-personal-video first-action constraint unless it contains a useful offline demonstration. A richer interactive video example best teaches end-to-end behavior, yet bundled media, deterministic local analysis, accessible media description and result interpretation could exceed the week (evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051).

The key unresolved feasibility question for the team is whether the app already has a representative, accessible local result/sample path, or whether a precomputed synthetic result would misrepresent actual analysis. This also responds to Maya's and Grace's uncertainty about whether output comprehension, file choice, or privacy is the main barrier: the supplied brief establishes only an empty-workspace first-action problem, not a measured diagnosis. Accessibility validation still missing: keyboard-only navigation and activation (including focus order/return and visible focus), screen-reader names and announcement of changed content, and whether the sample/result's meaning is available in text rather than imagery alone, checked on the actual target platform with assistive technology. No user testing, analytics, interviews, or confirmed accessibility evaluation are in the supplied brief; no public research was used.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_8664f12b-c22a-4d04-b088-a896ab87f8b0","cmessage_093e3434-e152-47ad-835d-0d908fa20d3a","cmessage_b30fe453-d7ed-4b06-b42f-18a6e956b6c3","cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051"],"answers":[],"questions":[{"question":"Does a representative local sample/result path already exist, and can its meaning be presented accessibly without building a full video-processing demo?","target_worker_id":"worker_0e23d704-3661-49b1-a05d-0d4f3cdb5d77"}]}

{"body":"The new owner constraint changes the baseline: “Choose a video” alone cannot be the first useful action, because it requires importing personal media (cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6). I recommend a small, keyboard-reachable “Explore an example result” action in the empty workspace, using bundled synthetic/example data and a text-first, screen-reader-readable result explanation that works offline; put “Open your own video on this device” beside it as an optional next step with a clear no-upload statement. This is a constrained interactive-example *entry point* plus contextual help, not a full guided tour or a promise of live sample-video processing. It gives a useful first action without network or personal import while keeping the proposed scope closer to one engineer-week. If even a prepackaged example result cannot be represented meaningfully with existing UI in that week, the team should not claim this meets the new constraint; the feasibility decision remains open.

Trade-offs: a guided setup could explain privacy and prerequisites but adds navigation/focus/state burden and still needs a no-personal-video useful endpoint. A fully interactive synthetic clip could teach the actual workflow better, yet packaging, deterministic offline analysis, media controls, accessible alternatives, and result narration make the one-week fit uncertain. Contextual copy alone is cheapest, but reading instructions without a meaningful sample interaction may not satisfy “first useful action.” Maya’s empty-state emphasis remains right for discoverability (cmessage_093e3434-e152-47ad-835d-0d908fa20d3a), while Grace’s conditional sample path addresses the revised constraint more directly (cmessage_b30fe453-d7ed-4b06-b42f-18a6e956b6c3). I would prefer a prepackaged, inspectable example result over promising full sample processing unless that pipeline already exists.

Answer to Maya’s question: the supplied brief does not establish a reliable sample-analysis path or accessible result representation, so I cannot assume either exists. The owner-supplied hypothetical brief describes an empty workspace and no analytics, interviews, or confirmed accessibility evaluation, not implementation readiness (evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051). Accessibility validation still missing: keyboard-only traversal/activation and return path; visible focus; screen-reader names, reading order, and status/result announcement; text equivalents for any visual/video-only insight; and offline verification with network disabled. These are proposed checks, not tests performed. The key team question is whether a meaningful, accessible prepackaged example result can be delivered within the week using existing components; if not, what narrower non-video first action counts as genuinely useful under the owner constraint?","contribution_ids":["cmessage_093e3434-e152-47ad-835d-0d908fa20d3a","cmessage_b30fe453-d7ed-4b06-b42f-18a6e956b6c3","cmessage_f1c6a163-5334-45f9-9c00-80a6ce1eadb6"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051"],"answers":["question_27419915-68a2-4394-aea2-9da8e2b1677b"],"questions":[{"question":"Can an existing result view meaningfully display a bundled, text-accessible synthetic example offline within one engineer-week, without building a new analysis path?","target_worker_id":null}]}

{"body":"My first-version recommendation is a compact, accessible empty-state choice: a prominent “Open a video on this device” action, a short statement that analysis stays local, and a secondary “Try a sample” path only if a bundled synthetic clip and its analysis can be delivered accessibly within the week. Add concise, task-specific hints beside the first action/result rather than a separate tour. This directly addresses the supplied empty-workspace problem without requiring users to surrender their own media or complete a multi-step gate. It is a proposal, not a tested outcome.

Trade-offs: guided setup offers a clear sequence and can explain permissions, but a full wizard risks consuming the engineer-week, trapping repeat users, and creating extra keyboard/focus and screen-reader obligations. An interactive example could teach the complete workflow without exposing private videos and may be strongest pedagogically; however, bundled media, deterministic offline processing, accessible controls/transcript or equivalent, and a clear way back to one's own file enlarge scope. Contextual help is cheapest and least intrusive, but help attached to an otherwise empty workspace may not make the first action salient. Thus I would make the first-action empty state the baseline and treat the sample as conditional, not promise all three patterns.

Accessibility is a release constraint, not polish: the primary and secondary actions should be keyboard reachable with visible focus and meaningful names; instructions and analysis output should be conveyed in text rather than only by video imagery or hover. The brief reports no confirmed accessibility evaluation, analytics, or interviews, so neither adoption gains nor feasibility are established. Important team question: is the main first-use barrier choosing a local file, understanding what the analysis will produce, or trusting that the file stays on-device? The answer changes whether we spend the scarce week on clearer entry copy, the synthetic example, or privacy explanation. A second feasibility unknown is whether a genuinely accessible bundled example already exists; without it, favor the lean empty state plus contextual guidance.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_8664f12b-c22a-4d04-b088-a896ab87f8b0"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051"],"answers":[],"questions":[{"question":"Which first-use uncertainty should be prioritized: selecting a local file, understanding the output, or trusting the no-upload behavior?","target_worker_id":null}]}

{"body":"My opening recommendation is a focused empty-state onboarding first version: in the existing workspace, state the first local action plainly, offer a keyboard-operable “Choose a video” control, explain that analysis stays on-device, and provide short contextual guidance at the point of choice. Treat this as contextual help rather than a tour or multi-step setup. It directly addresses the supplied problem—an empty workspace with no obvious first action—while keeping scope plausible for one engineer-week and avoiding any upload path (charter cmessage_8664f12b-c22a-4d04-b088-a896ab87f8b0; hypothetical brief evidence_61d51f75-cdd2-4561-bb71-e1570d101051). This is a design hypothesis, not a tested outcome or effort estimate.

Trade-offs: Guided setup can make prerequisites and privacy expectations explicit, but a modal/wizard adds state, dismissal/re-entry, and keyboard/focus complexity before users can act. An interactive example with bundled synthetic media could teach the result and spare users from choosing private media; it is attractive if an existing local sample-analysis path is already dependable. But packaging, compatibility, and an accessible explanation of the analysis output can exceed the week, and a synthetic example may not answer how to use one’s own file. Contextual help is smallest and least obstructive, but may underteach what results mean; it should not bury the primary action in a tooltip or rely on hover/visual cues. Keyboard order, visible focus, clear control names, and screen-reader-readable status/results are acceptance concerns, not assumed to be solved merely by adding copy.

